oob-usb-serial (2.0.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Major refactor from GNU screen to tio + tmux.
    - tmux (dedicated socket) is the multiplexer: one detached session with one
      named window per adapter; remain-on-exit keeps closed consoles visible;
      Ctrl-b R respawns a console.
    - tio is the serial client per window, launched on the adapter's stable
      /dev/serial/by-id path for correct auto-reconnect across replugs.
  * BREAKING: devices.conf replaces the stty-style `framing` key with tio-native
    per-device keys data/parity/stop/flow; global defaults become
    OOB_DEFAULT_BAUD/DATA/PARITY/STOP/FLOW. Legacy `framing` keys are ignored.
  * Session commands (start/stop/attach/status) always act on the OOB_USER's
    tmux server. `tmux -L` sockets are per-user, so the CLI re-executes itself
    as OOB_USER when invoked as root (runuser, fallback sudo -u); this prevents
    each user spawning a separate, colliding tmux server.
  * Discovery gains the /dev/serial/by-id path; optional OOB_LOG_DIR enables
    per-console logging.
  * There is no `interactive` subcommand: a serial port has a single
    reader/writer, so consoles are only ever opened via the one tmux session
    (attach with `oob-usb-serial attach`).
  * Dependencies: screen replaced by tio and tmux.
  * Detailed design/configuration in docs/DESIGN.md.

 -- diaznet <diaznet@users.noreply.github.com>  Tue, 01 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0000

oob-usb-serial (1.0.3) unstable; urgency=medium

  * interactive: add a framing menu applied via stty before the console opens.
    Choose an editable preset (from
    /usr/share/oob-usb-serial/serial_framings.txt) or "Custom" to set data
    bits, parity, stop bits and flow control individually.

